1125:
A. Each and every property owner wishing to utilize the provisions of this chapter may do so only after filing an application with the Community Development Department and receiving approval thereof. Such application shall consist of three phases. The first phase consists of payment of a nonrefundable inspection fee for each building anticipated for residential use and performance by the city of an inspection of each building anticipated for such use, which shall result in a detailed list of required improvements, if any, which must be made prior to final approval. The Enforcement Authority shall take the following into consideration in determining required improvements:
1. Hazardous Conditions. With reference to existing buildings, enforcement authorities should assure that any of the conditions or defects described in this section are identified and corrected in a matter deemed reasonably appropriate by the enforcement authority through the utilization the applicable minimum standards or, if necessary, of appropriate compliance alternatives:
a. Structural. Any building or structure or portion thereof which is in imminent danger of collapse because of, but not limited to the following factors:
(1) Dilapidation, deterioration, or decay;
(2) Faulty structural design or construction;
(3) The removal, movement, or instability of any portion of the ground necessary for the purpose of supporting such building; or
(4) The deterioration, decay, or inadequacy of the foundation.
b. Number of exits. Less than two (2) approved independent, remote and properly protected exit ways serving every story of a building, except where a single exit way is permitted by the applicable state or local fire or building code or life safety codes; and if acceptable alternative safeguards cannot be met, an automatic fire suppression system shall be provided.
c. Capacity of exists. Any required door, aisle, passageway, stairway, or other means of egress which is not of sufficient capacity to serve the population of the portions of the building served and which is not so arranged as to provide safe and adequate means of egress to a place of safety.
d. Mechanical Systems. Utilities and mechanical systems not in conformance with the codes in effect at the time of construction of a building which creates a serious threat of fire or threaten the safety of the occupants of the building.
2. Additions to Existing Building. Additions to an existing building shall comply with the applicable requirements of state and local laws, rules, regulations, codes, and standards for new construction. Such additions shall not impose upon existing structure loads either vertical or horizontal which would cause the existing building to be subjected to stresses exceeding those that would be permitted under new construction. If the existing building does not comply with the standards provided in this section and the authorized enforcement authority finds that the addition adversely affects the performance of the total building, the authorized enforcement authority may require:
a. The new addition to be separated from the existing structure by at least a two-hour fire wall with openings therein properly protected; or
b. The installation of an approved automatic fire suppression system; or
c. Other remedies which may be deemed appropriate by the enforcement authority.
3. Minor Alterations and New Mechanical Systems. Minor alterations or repairs to an existing building which do not adversely affect the performance or safety of the building may be made with the same or like materials. Existing buildings which, in part or as a whole, exceed the requirements of any applicable construction or fire safety code, may, in the course of compliance with this chapter, have reduced or removed, in part or total, features not required by such code for new construction; provided, however, that such features were not a specific condition of prior approval. Existing buildings and structures which, in part or as a whole, do not meet the requirements of the then existing code for new construction may be altered or repaired without further compliance to any such code by utilizing the provisions of this chapter, provided their present degree of compliance to any applicable then existing construction of or fire safety code is not reduced. Any new mechanical systems installed in an existing building shall conform to then existing codes for new construction to the fullest extent practical, except as approved by the authorized enforcement authorities pursuant to the authority of this chapter.
4. Floor Loading. Any proposed change in the use or occupancy of an existing building or portion thereof which could increase the floor loading should be investigated by a Georgia registered professional architect/engineer to determine the adequacy of the existing floor systems to support the estimated proposed increased loads. If the existing floor system is found to be inadequate, it should be modified to support the increased loads or the proposed allowable floor loading shall be reduced by the appropriate enforcement authority and the building shall be posted by the appropriate enforcement authority so as to unlawful use.
5. Compliance Alternatives. It is recognized for purposes of this chapter that all building systems interact with each other; therefore, any consideration of compliance alternatives should take into account all existing and proposed conditions to determine the acceptability of any compliance alternatives. The compliance alternatives are not all-inclusive and do not preclude consideration and approval of other alternatives by any enforcement authority.
a. Compliance alternatives for an inadequate number of exits include, but are not limited to:
(1) Provision of connecting fire-exit balconies between buildings, acceptable to the enforcement authority;
(2) Provision of alternative exits or egress facilities leading to safety outside the building or to a place of safe refuge in the building or an adjoining building as acceptable to the enforcement authority;
(3) Provision of an exterior fire escape or escapes as acceptable to the enforcement authority where the providing of enclosed interior or enclosed exterior stairs is not practical; or
(4) Installation of early fire warning and fire suppression systems.
b. Compliance alternatives for excessive travel distances to an approved exit include:
(1) Installation of an approved smoke detection system throughout the building;
(2) Installation of an approved completed automatic fire suppression system;
(3) Subdivision of the exit travel route with smoke-stop doors acceptable to the enforcement authority;
(4) Increase in the fire resistance rating of corridor walls and doors; or
(5) Provision of additional means of escape.
c. Compliance alternatives for unenclosed or improperly enclosed exit stairways or vertical shafts include:
(1) Improvement of enclosure of exit stairway;
(2) Addition of a partial fire suppression system;
(3) Addition of a sprinkler draft curtain; or
(4) Addition of a smoke detection system.
d. Compliance alternatives for inadequate or a total lack of fire partitions or fire separations walls shall be as set forth in subsection (c) above.
e. Compliance alternatives for a lack of required protection of openings in exterior walls where a fire exposure is a risk include:
(1) Improvement of fire resistance of existing openings and protect them protection with fire-related windows or doors as appropriate;
(2) Sealing of the openings with fire-related construction as approved by the enforcement authority; or
(3) Installation of an approved fire suppression system.
B. The second phase consists of submittal of the following:
1. A survey of the property showing the existing structures and their proximity to all other structures on the site and structures off the site lying within thirty (30) feet;
2. An area map showing all adjacent rights-of-way and lots with current ownership and uses;
3. A floor plan of the building which is to be used partially or completely as a residential structure showing the following items: all existing exterior and interior walls, noting walls to be removed, walls to remain or walls to be added;
4. A parking plan, which depicts how off-street parking will be provided. It shall show a minimum of one permanent space for each six hundred (600) square feet of living space or increment thereof. This minimum requirement shall be in addition to the requirements of section 17.20.020 for off-street parking for the underlying primary use;
5. A construction schedule within which deficiencies identified in Phase I, if any, are anticipated to be corrected and other improvements are to be made;
6. Payment of a nonrefundable plan review fee and associated building permit and trades permits, if any; and
7. Upon plan review, issuance by the city of associated permits.
C. Phase III shall consist of performance of the work in accordance with the issued permit(s) and progress inspections by the city of completed work. Upon completion of the work, a certificate of conditional residential occupancy shall be issued. Such permit shall be conditioned upon the nature and extent of work performed and shall limit the number and type of residential occupancy in accordance with the accommodations provided. Subsequent occupancy or tenancy of the building shall be similarly controlled unless subsequent increase or reduction in residential-oriented space is made. Any occupancy of the building found to be in noncompliance with the limitations contained in the conditional residency permit shall be in violation of the code and unlawful.
